---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/intermediate/customers-and-markets">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Markets are <strong>groups of people with a shared problem and budget</strong> — and business
    strategy begins by choosing which group to serve and how to be remembered by it. Segmentation,
    targeting, positioning (STP) is the sequence; product–market fit is the verdict.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="segment, target, position — and how to know when it worked" widget="equationStep" config={custCfg} />

  <h2>Market analysis tools that earn their keep</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>TAM/SAM/SOM:</strong> total, serviceable and obtainable market — sizing honestly separates fundable ideas from wishful arithmetic.</li>
    <li><strong>Market research done cheaply:</strong> customer interviews ("tell me about the last time you…", never "would you use…"), competitor teardowns, search-volume data. Opinions about the future are noise; past behaviour is signal.</li>
    <li><strong>Network-effect markets are different:</strong> winner-take-most dynamics (marketplaces, social platforms) mean speed and initial liquidity matter more than features — a different strategy entirely.</li>
    <li><strong>The marketing lesson next door</strong> takes STP into tactics: price, promotion, place and product decisions — the 4Ps.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kotler & Keller, Marketing Management; Christensen et al., Competing Against Luck; Andreessen, "The Only Thing That Matters" (2010).</p>
</TopicPage>
